text:
alice: Heads-up — noticed huge retry storms on our Kotlin sample when running on older Android emulator (api 23). Calls stall, then multiple retries fire and auth server returns 429. Physical devices not hit as badly.

alice: Repro: Retrofit suspend endpoint + an Interceptor that does a blocking refresh using `runBlocking` if token expired. On emulator the app exhausts OkHttp dispatcher threads and other requests queue up, creating concurrent refresh attempts.

alice: Example problematic Interceptor: ```kotlin
class RefreshInterceptor : Interceptor {
  override fun intercept(chain: Interceptor.Chain): Response {
    val req = chain.request()
    if (tokenExpired()) {
      // blocking on coroutine in interceptor thread
      val newToken = runBlocking { tokenService.refresh() }
      return chain.proceed(req.newBuilder().header("Authorization","Bearer $newToken").build())
    }
    return chain.proceed(req)
  }
}
```

ben: oy. `runBlocking` inside an OkHttp dispatcher thread is dicey. On older VMs the dispatcher threads are small and coroutine dispatcher may starve, causing deadlock or long hangs. Combined with our retry layer, this triggers many duplicate refresh attempts.

dina: saw similar in a customer report where background uploads stalled and then retry loop multiplied requests. Two quick fixes: 1) move refresh out of interceptor and use non-blocking suspend flow upstream; 2) make the refresh client use a dedicated Executor that won't compete with OkHttp dispatcher.

jonah: approach we used in samples: provide an `AuthTokenProvider` with a non-blocking check that enqueues a single refresh and lets other callers await the result without calling network on the OkHttp thread. Example pattern: a synchronized cache + CompletableDeferred tokenRefresh in Kotlin coroutines.

jonah: rough snippet: ```kotlin
class TokenProvider(private val api: AuthApi) {
  private val lock = Mutex()
  private var refreshInProgress: CompletableDeferred<String>? = null
  suspend fun getToken(): String {
    if (!isExpired()) return current
    return lock.withLock {
      refreshInProgress?.let { return it.await() }
      val d = CompletableDeferred<String>()
      refreshInProgress = d
      try {
        val t = api.refresh() // suspend network call on IO
        current = t
        d.complete(t)
        return t
      } catch (e: Throwable) { d.completeExceptionally(e); throw e }
      finally { refreshInProgress = null }
    }
  }
}
```

alice: we put an example like that into samples/android/auth-concurrency and removed `runBlocking`. Emulator hangs vanished and duplicate refreshes dropped to zero.

ben: for customers who can't lift to suspend-based pipelines, we recommend using a synchronous refresh client with a dedicated OkHttp client + executor to avoid competing for the main dispatcher's threads. Eg: `OkHttpClient.Builder().dispatcher(Dispatcher(Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor())).build()` for the auth client.

dina: SDK doc idea: "Don't block OkHttp dispatcher threads with `runBlocking` or long sync refreshes". Provide three remedies: non-blocking token provider (samples), dedicated auth client with its own dispatcher, or pre-refresh tokens before request.

jonah: also a small SDK improvement: detect if an Interceptor calls `runBlocking` (hard to do) — easier: add a runtime debug check to warn if total active dispatcher queued calls > threshold and recommend auth concurrency patterns. We can emit a one-time log when retry storms are detected.

alice: opened samples PR and a docs PR with the token provider pattern and the dedicated auth client snippet. ciara-bot kicked off CI.

ciara-bot: CI started for branch alice/auth-provider-sample : emulator matrix (api23, api29) running.

ben: tiny extra — for Retrofit with suspend functions, make sure not to call `runBlocking` from UI or OkHttp threads; prefer `withContext(Dispatchers.IO)` & structured concurrency. Add that to onboarding checklist.

dina: agreed. I'll add a short FAQ entry "Why does my app hang on older Android/emulator when using runBlocking in Interceptor?" with examples and fix links.

